       begin
          bufferin_write_switch <= ~bufferin_write_switch;
          if (bufferin_write_switch)
            bufferin_full1_A <= ~bufferin_full1_A;
          else
            bufferin_full0_A <= ~bufferin_full0_A;
       end
  end
```

So when bufferin_addr reaches N-1 (all ones, `&bufferin_addr` is true), the NEXT sample wraps to 0 and the write switch flips. So after filling a buffer with N samples, the writer switches to the other buffer and marks the just-filled buffer's `_A` flag.

The `&bufferin_addr` is the reduction-AND: true when all bits are 1, i.e., addr = N-1.

So:
- writer writes N samples into one buffer, then toggles write_switch and flips that buffer's _A flag.

The read switch (`bufferin_read_switch`): selects which buffer reads come from. Lines 376-384:
```verilog
if (first_stage)
  begin
     if (bufferin_read_switch)
