     else
       bufferin_full0_B <= ~bufferin_full0_B;
     bufferin_read_switch <= ~bufferin_read_switch;
  end
```

This is in the FSM_ST_CALC state when `&(out1_addr)` (the last butterfly of the stage). So when the first stage finishes reading all of one input buffer, the read switch flips and that buffer's _B flag flips.

`bufferin_read_full = bufferin_read_switch?bufferin_full1:bufferin_full0` — the buffer currently being read is full.
`bufferin_write_full = bufferin_write_switch?bufferin_full1:bufferin_full0` — the buffer currently being written is full.

The overflow detection: `if (bufferin_write_full) overflow <= 1'b1;` — if the writer tries to write into a buffer that's already full (read hasn't consumed it yet), set overflow.

So the double buffering: while the FSM reads from one input buffer to perform the first stage, the input port can write into the other buffer. This is the parallelism.

